   323	Corollary 4.8. Let (f, g) be a Jacobain pair. The following are equivalent.
   324	    (i) K(f, g) = K(x, y).
   325	   (ii) K[f, g] = K[x, y].
   326	  (iii) I(f, g) = 1.
   327	  (iv) f has no minor roots.
   328	   (v) I(f, fy ) = degy f − 1.
   329	                                                            6
   330	Proof. Let ξ and η be symbols, let fξ = f − ξ, gη = g − η. And let F (ξ, η, x) = Resy (fξ , gη ). Then
   331	F (ξ, η, x) is irreducible ([Moh83] page 150) and F (f (x, y), g(x, y), x) = 0. So F (f, g, X) is the define
   332	equation of x over K(f, g). Thus I(fξ , gµ ) is the degree of field extension [K(x, y) : K(f, g)], which
   333	is equal to 1 if and only if there is no minor root from above theorem. We also see from the proof
   334	of the last theorem, if there is no minor root, then the coefficient of the leading term of F (ξ, µ, x)
   335	in x is in K∗ . The corollary is thus proved.                                                             
   336	Corollary 4.9. ([Xu93]) Let f (x, y) be a polynomial, monic in y. If f is an embedded line, then
   337	I(f, fy ) = degy f − 1. In other words, the curve f has degy f − 1 vertical tangent lines.
   338	Proof. By Abhyankar-Moh’s Epimorphism Theorem ([AM75]), there exits polynomial g(x, y) such
   339	that Jacobian J(f, g) ∈ K∗ and K[x, y] = K[f, g]. By above corollary, I(f, fy ) = degy f − 1.
