   618	                    8. Case (99, 66) and split of principal minor roots
   619	  Moh writes in [Moh83] page 209 on the principal minor roots, “Namely, there is an [sic] unique
   620	π-root σ of g(y) of the following form
   621	                                      σ = a−1 t−1 + a0 + a1 t + πt2
   622	with
   623	                        g(σ) = gπ (σ)t−18 + · · ·     [sic, gπ (σ) should be gσ (π)]
   624	The polynomial gσ (π) is either a power of a linear polynomial or the 9-th power of a cubic polynomial
   625	with precisely two roots.”
   626	  For a long time, we try to give judgement for this statement. To do so, there are three claims
   627	have to be proven.
   628	     (i) The principal minor roots do not split except at π-root order δ = 2.
   629	    (ii) gσ (π) can not have three roots when δ = 2.
   630	   (iii) Let δ = 2 and let gσ (π) = c(π − b1 )18 (π − b2 )9 . Roots in the disc with the center σ|π=b1 do
   631	         not split before final.
   632	                                                      12
   633	We can not find materials to support these claims in his paper. We sent Moh an email to point
   634	out the possible gaps. He replied with, “I will make an investigation of the issue and reply to your
   635	e-mail as soon as possible.” on Jan. 06, 2016.
   636	  We show that (ii) and (iii) are true, but there is one exception case for (i) open.
