   152	## 1. The tower, the Theorem-1.2 order rows, and the chart
   153	
   154	### 1.1 The tower is canonical
   155	
   156	In char 0 the `d`-th *approximate root* of monic `Q in k[x][y]` of `y`-degree `N`,
   157	`d | N`, is the unique monic `h` of `y`-degree `N/d` with
   158	`deg_y(Q - h^d) < N - N/d` (Abhyankar-Moh; Moh sec 1). Put
   159	
   160	```text
   161	h2 := 3rd approximate root of F  (y-degree 33),   h3 := 3rd approximate root of h2  (11).
   162	```
   163	
   164	Both exist and are unique; no choice is made. `h3` is then the 9th approximate
   165	root of `F`, i.e. a quasi-approximate root in the sense of Moh Definition 1.5,
   166	which is what Theorem 1.2 requires.
   167	
   168	**Lemma 1.1.** `deg h2 = deg_y h2 = 33` and `deg h3 = deg_y h3 = 11`.
   169	
   170	*Proof.* The approximate root is given by universal polynomials with rational
   171	coefficients in the coefficients of `Q` (only division by `d` occurs), so with
   172	`(sigma_lambda Q)(x,y) = lambda^{-N}Q(lambda x, lambda y)` -- again monic in `y`
   173	of `y`-degree `N`, with coefficients polynomial in `lambda^{-1}` -- one has
   174	`A_d(sigma_lambda Q) = lambda^{-N/d}(A_d Q)(lambda x, lambda y)`. The left side is
   175	bounded as `lambda -> infinity`; the right side is bounded only if
   176	`deg A_d Q <= N/d`. As `A_d Q` is monic of `y`-degree `N/d`, equality holds. QED
   177	
   178	### 1.2 The chart is a bijection
   179	
   180	**Proposition 1.2.** With `P = y^3(y-x)^8` and
   181	`S(D,r) = {A : deg A <= D, deg_y A < r}`, `dim S(D,r) = r(D+1) - r(r-1)/2`, the map
   182	
   183	```text
   184	h3 = P + H,              H  in S(10,11)   66
   185	h2 = h3^3 + C2 h3 + C3,  C2 in S(21,11)  187 ,  C3 in S(32,11)  308
   186	F  = h2^3 + A2 h2 + A3,  A2 in S(65,33) 1650 ,  A3 in S(98,33) 2739
   187	G  = h2^2 + B1 h2 + B2,  B1 in S(32,33)  561 ,  B2 in S(65,33) 1650
   188	```
   189	
   190	is a bijection from `k^{7161}` onto the set of pairs `(F,G)` with
   191	`deg F = deg_y F = 99`, `deg G = deg_y G = 66`, both monic in `y`, `in F = P^9`,
   192	`in G = P^6`.
   193	
   194	*Proof.* (Into.) `deg H <= 10 < 11` gives `in h3 = P`, `deg h3 = 11`; then
   195	`deg(C2h3) <= 32`, `deg C3 <= 32 < 33` give `in h2 = P^3`, `deg h2 = 33`; then
   196	`deg(A2h2) <= 98`, `deg A3 <= 98 < 99` give `in F = P^9`; and `deg(B1h2) <= 65`,
   197	`deg B2 <= 65` give `in G = P^6`.
   198	
   199	(Onto, injective.) Put `h2 = A_3(F)`, `h3 = A_3(h2)`; degrees 33, 11 by
   200	Lemma 1.1. Since `deg_y(F - h2^3) <= 65 < 99`, the degree-99 forms agree, so
   201	`(in h2)^3 = P^9`, hence `in h2 = P^3` (unique monic cube root of a form in
   202	char 0) and likewise `in h3 = P`; so `H = h3 - P` lies in `S(10,11)`. Next
   203	`F - h2^3` has total degree `<= 98` (top forms cancel) and `y`-degree `<= 65`;
   204	dividing by `h2`, monic with `deg h2 = deg_y h2 = 33`, each step subtracts
   205	`c(x)y^{j-33}h2` of degree `<= (98-j)+(j-33)+33 = 98`, so `deg A3 <= 98`,
   206	`deg A2 <= 65`, both of `y`-degree `<= 32`. The same argument on
   207	`deg(G-h2^2) <= 65` and on `deg(h2-h3^3) <= 32`, `deg_y(h2-h3^3) <= 21` gives
   208	`B1 in S(32,33)`, `B2 in S(65,33)`, `C2 in S(21,11)`, `C3 in S(32,11)`. Each step
   209	is division with remainder by a monic polynomial, hence unique. QED
