   476	## 4. The minor-incidence block, per branch
   477	
   478	### 4.1 The branch datum and its necessity
   479	
   480	Fix a branch `delta in {2, 5/2}` (sec 7). After the gauges of sec 2, with
   481	`t = x^{-1}`:
   482	
   483	```text
   484	delta = 2   :  y = u t + z t^2,                     w = y t = u t^2 + z t^3
   485	delta = 5/2 :  t = s^2, y = u s^2 + v s^4 + pi s^5, w = u s^4 + v s^6 + pi s^7
   486	```
   487	
   488	leaders `p = pi^2(pi+3a)`, `a != 0` (`[2,1]`) and `p = pi(pi^2-c)`, `c != 0`
   489	(`[1,1,1]`). Xu sec 8 records `f(sigma) = p^6 t^{6(-8+3delta)} + ...`,
   490	`g(sigma) = p^9 t^{9(-8+3delta)} + ...`, `T2(sigma) = p^5 t^{5(-8+3delta)} + ...`,
   491	`T3(sigma) = q(pi)t^{13(-8+3delta)-1+delta} + ...`, `deg p = u_s = 3`,
   492	`deg q = 13u_s+1 = 40`. At `delta = 2` this gives `ord F(sigma) = 9(6-8) = -18`,
   493	`ord G(sigma) = -12`, i.e. exactly Moh p.209's
   494	`g(sigma) = g_sigma(pi)t^{-18} + ...`, so
   495	
   496	```text
   497	t^{18} F(t^{-1},sigma) = p^9 + O(t),     t^{12} G(t^{-1},sigma) = p^6 + O(t).
   498	```
   499	
   500	These are *equalities* of Laurent expansions; they say two necessary things:
   501	
   502	1. **Pole rows.** All coefficients below the leader vanish. Homogenised,
   503	   `t^{18}F(t^{-1},sigma) = t^{-81}KF` and `t^{12}G = t^{-54}KG`, so the
   504	   coefficient at pole exponent `-81+n` is `[t^n]KF`; `[t^0]KF = 0` automatically
   505	   (the homogenised top form `w^{27}(w-1)^{72}` vanishes at `w = 0`), so the rows
   506	   start at `n = 1`. The leader sits at `n = 63` (exponent `-18`), so 62 such
   507	   vanishing rows exist for `F`; the engine used eight. For `delta = 5/2`, `s^9F(s^{-2},sigma) = s^{-189}KF`,
