   719	`ord F(sigma) = (n/d_s)(u_s delta - v_s) = 9(3delta-8)`; a root at infinity has
   720	`ord F(sigma) < 0`, so `delta < v_s/u_s = 8/3`.
   721	
   722	*Step 2 (denominator).* Xu sec 8: "our tools are two constrains: one is that the
   723	denominator of order `delta <= u_s = 3`". So `den(delta) in {1,2,3}`, and
   724	
   725	*Step 3 (candidates)* the rationals with `1 < delta < 8/3`, `den <= 3` are exactly
   726	`{4/3, 3/2, 5/3, 2, 7/3, 5/2}`.
   727	
   728	*Step 4 (how many parts).* Xu Cor 7.5: if `u_s > 1` and
   729	`delta < (v_s+1)/(u_s+1) = 9/4`, `sigma` cannot split into `u_s = 3` different
   730	roots; so `{4/3, 3/2, 5/3, 2}` admit only a two-part split, `{7/3, 5/2}` two or
   731	three.
   732	
   733	*Step 5 (the ODE).* Xu sec 8(i): "When `delta != 2` and `delta != 5/2`. From
   734	equation 7.1, we can step by step deduce `q(pi) = p(pi)^{13}(pi - c)` and the
   735	equation 7.1 can not hold for it." This removes `4/3, 3/2, 5/3, 7/3`. At
   736	`delta = 2` Xu exhibits the surviving solution, display (8.2) -- verified in
   737	sec 4.3 -- which is Moh's two-root split, the three-root alternative being
   738	excluded by Step 4 (Xu sec 8(ii)). At `delta = 5/2` Xu deduces `q = p^{10}q_1`
   739	with `q_1' = -2p^3`, which "can hold for any `p(pi)`", and records
   740	`p = pi(pi^2-c)`: three distinct roots. `den(5/2) = 2`, and the Galois
   741	automorphism `s -> -s` of `t = s^2` must permute the parts, consistently with
   742	`p = pi(pi^2-c)` (it exchanges `+-sqrt(c)` and fixes 0).
   743	
   744	*Step 6 (face gauge).* `pi -> lambda pi` sends `a -> lambda a`,
   745	`c -> lambda^2 c`, so a nonzero branch parameter may be normalised; the engine
   746	does not use this and keeps `rho`, `c` symbolic.
   747	
